\begin{cases} a + 3b = 0 \\ 2a + kb = 0 \end{cases}
\] For the vectors to be linearly dependent, the system must have a non-trivial solution (i.e., \(a\) and \(b\) are not both zero). This happens if and only if the determinant of the coefficient matrix is zero. The coefficient matrix is \[
\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 2 & k \end{pmatrix}
\] The determinant of this matrix is \(1 \cdot k - 2 \cdot 3 = k - 6\). Setting the determinant to zero, we get \(k - 6 = 0\), so \(k = 6\). Therefore, the set of vectors is linearly dependent if and only if \(k = 6\). </think> <answer> 6 </answer>

